# Why a straw looks bent in water
subtitle: "Follow one ray of light out of the water to see why a straight straw looks broken, and why a pool's bottom looks shallower than it is."

## Why a straw looks bent in water
use: refraction
with:
    medium1: water
    n1: 1.33
    medium2: air
    n2: 1
    light_from: below
    theta1_deg: 30
    apparent: true
    eye: true
    source_label: straw tip
    show_reflection: false
    show_critical: false

    card title "The bent straw"

Why does a straw standing in a glass of water look broken, right at the surface?
    draw media boundary

The straw itself is perfectly straight.
    path straw #8a5fa8 points [[390,209.5],[255,495.3]] style.opacity 0.85 style.stroke_width 9

Follow the light from one point on it: its tip, under the water.
    draw source source_label

That light travels up toward the surface in a straight line.
    draw incident_ray

Measured from the dashed line square to the surface, it arrives at thirty degrees.
    draw normal theta1_arc theta1_label

Crossing into air, it bends away from that line.
    draw refracted_ray

It leaves at almost forty-two degrees.
    draw theta2_arc theta2_label

Light moves about a third faster in air than in water, and the side of the beam that gets out first pulls ahead, swinging the beam round.
    highlight n1_label n2_label

Now put your eye where that light lands.
    draw eye

Your brain assumes light travels in straight lines, so it traces the ray straight back.
    draw apparent_ray

That line points above the real tip: the tip looks shallower than it is.
    draw image image_label

    fade incident_ray refracted_ray apparent_ray to 0.35

Every point below the surface is lifted the same way, while the part in the air stays put, so the straw seems to kink at the surface.
    path straw_seen #8a5fa8 points [[390,276.5],[300,400]] style.opacity 0.4 style.stroke_width 9

The straw was never bent; the light was.
    highlight straw

    erase straw straw_seen

    fade incident_ray refracted_ray apparent_ray to 1

Look from a lower angle, and the tip seems to rise even higher.
    animate theta1_deg 44 duration 3

Now drag the tip or the ray yourself, and watch where the tip appears.
    explore

    quiz "Looking into a pool, where does a coin on the bottom seem to be?"
        right: Shallower: its light bends away from the normal as it leaves the water, and traced straight back it points above the coin.
        wrong: Think about which way the light bends as it leaves the water.
        * Deeper than it is
        + Shallower than it is
        * Exactly where it is
---
